<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null) => (this.get(index) == null || this.get(index).equals(element));</code>

However, the above assertion seems to have a logical error. The correct interpretation of the comment should be that if the element is added at a valid index, then either the element is null and the element at that index becomes null, or the element at that index equals the inserted element. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null) ? (this.get(index) == null) : this.get(index).equals(element);</code>

But since the `get(index)` after adding an element at `index` will actually be the newly added element, the correct assertion should be:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null) ? (this.get(index) == null) : this.get(index).equals(element);</code>

This can be simplified to:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => this.get(index).equals(element);</code>

Since if the element is null, `this.get(index)` will also be null after insertion.

Therefore, the final correct assertion is:

<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => this.get(index).equals(element);</code>
